WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
On a hot, dry, windy afternoon, a plant

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.200
in a pot can lose water from its

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.600
leaves faster than its roots can take it

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.200
up.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.880
It has no brain, no nerves and no

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.600
blood.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.400
Yet within minutes, its leaves can cut that

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.360
loss.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.840
So how does a plant keep enough water?

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.066
Start with where the water goes.

00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:24.186
Water travels up from the roots in xylem,

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.026
the plant's water-carrying tubes.

00:00:26.507 --> 00:00:29.226
Inside a leaf, water evaporates from the wet

00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:31.946
walls of the cells into air spaces, so

00:00:32.027 --> 00:00:33.946
the air in there is very moist.

00:00:34.507 --> 00:00:37.306
That vapour diffuses out through tiny pores called

00:00:37.387 --> 00:00:40.186
stomata, mostly on the underside of the leaf.

00:00:40.747 --> 00:00:43.706
This loss of water vapour is transpiration.

00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:46.826
On a hot, dry, windy day the outside

00:00:46.907 --> 00:00:49.466
air is drier, and wind sweeps away the

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:51.786
moist air at the leaf surface, so water

00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:53.306
vapour leaves faster.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:56.933
So why not just keep the pores shut?

00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:00.293
Because the same stomata let carbon dioxide in,

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.733
and the leaf needs it for photosynthesis.

00:01:03.014 --> 00:01:06.133
Every open stoma is a deal: carbon dioxide

00:01:06.214 --> 00:01:08.133
in, water vapour out.

00:01:08.374 --> 00:01:10.533
A plant that never closed them could dry

00:01:10.614 --> 00:01:11.893
out on a hot day.

00:01:12.134 --> 00:01:14.453
One that never opened them would save water

00:01:14.614 --> 00:01:16.293
but couldn't make the sugar it needs to

00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:17.013
grow.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:21.273
Each stoma sits between two guard cells.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.673
Their walls are thicker on the side facing

00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:24.313
the pore.

00:01:24.874 --> 00:01:27.513
To open it, guard cells take in potassium

00:01:27.594 --> 00:01:30.233
ions, so the solution inside them gets more

00:01:30.354 --> 00:01:33.833
concentrated, and water follows by osmosis.

00:01:34.154 --> 00:01:37.193
The cells swell and become turgid, firm with

00:01:37.274 --> 00:01:37.753
water.

00:01:38.394 --> 00:01:41.113
Because the thick inner walls stretch less, the

00:01:41.194 --> 00:01:44.473
swollen cells bow apart, like two long balloons

00:01:44.554 --> 00:01:46.793
with tape along their inner edges, and the

00:01:46.874 --> 00:01:48.073
pore opens.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.740
Now the stress response.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.700
When the soil dries and the plant loses

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.340
water faster than it takes it up, cells

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.100
in the roots and leaves make more of

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.540
a hormone called abscisic acid, or ABA.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:05.060
ABA reaches the guard cells and makes potassium

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.180
ions leave them.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.740
Water follows the ions out by osmosis, the

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.180
guard cells go limp and straighten, and the

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.580
pore closes, often within minutes.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.540
So guard cells don't pump water.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.500
They move ions, and water follows.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.500
That's negative feedback, with the five parts you

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.300
know.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.660
The stimulus is the leaf's water content falling.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.660
The receptors are cells in the roots and

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.900
leaves that detect the shortage.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.540
There's no brain, so those same cells act

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.060
as the control, releasing ABA as the signal.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.460
The effectors are the guard cells.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.220
The response: stomata close, water loss falls, and

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.300
the water content recovers.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.700
The response opposes the fall.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.500
And when water is plentiful again, ABA levels

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.820
drop and the stomata can reopen.

00:02:56.034 --> 00:02:57.793
But closing has a cost.

00:02:58.274 --> 00:03:01.233
With the stomata shut, much less carbon dioxide

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:04.753
gets in, so photosynthesis slows and the plant

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:05.713
grows less.

00:03:06.274 --> 00:03:08.753
In a dry spell, it puts survival ahead

00:03:08.794 --> 00:03:09.393
of growth.

00:03:09.874 --> 00:03:12.353
And if the soil stays dry, closing may

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:13.313
not be enough.

00:03:13.794 --> 00:03:17.473
Water still escapes slowly, uptake keeps falling, the

00:03:17.554 --> 00:03:20.753
cells lose their firmness, and the leaves droop.

00:03:20.834 --> 00:03:21.793
That's wilting.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.480
Some plants live where dry is normal.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.120
Xerophytes, plants adapted to dry habitats, have savings

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.600
built into their leaves.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.240
A thick, waxy cuticle is a waterproof coat,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.640
so less water evaporates straight through the leaf

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:38.280
surface.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.720
Their stomata often sit sunken in pits, lined

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.720
with hairs.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.320
The pit holds a pocket of still, humid

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.840
air outside the pore.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.880
With moist air on both sides, the difference

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.400
in water vapour is smaller, so diffusion slows,

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.000
even while the pore is open.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.640
Australian plants show more of these.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.520
Spinifex has narrow, rolled leaves with stomata sheltered

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.400
in grooves, trapping humid air around them.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.920
Small or needle-like leaves have less surface to

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:09.880
lose water from.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.080
And many eucalypts hang their leaves vertically, so

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.520
the midday sun strikes them edge-on.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.760
The leaf stays cooler, so less water evaporates.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.520
To sort any feature, ask: does it waterproof

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.720
the surface, shrink it, keep it cool, or

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.080
trap humid air?

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.540
So how do these compare with ABA?

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.900
ABA closing the stomata is a response.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.020
It switches on when water runs short, works

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:40.020
within minutes and can reverse, but while the

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.180
stomata are shut, much less carbon dioxide gets

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:43.500
in.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.220
Xerophyte features are structural.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.260
They're always there and need no signal, so

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.860
they save water all day.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.100
But they can't be switched off when water

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.500
is plentiful, which is one reason many of

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.300
these plants grow slowly.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.220
Both keep the plant's water content within tolerance

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:00.940
limits.

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:03.526
Two exam questions.

00:05:04.087 --> 00:05:04.566
First.

00:05:05.047 --> 00:05:07.366
A potted wheat plant was not watered for

00:05:07.447 --> 00:05:08.526
six days.

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:11.686
The graph shows the ABA concentration in its

00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:14.006
leaves and its transpiration rate.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.686
Describe the relationship between the two, and explain

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:21.126
how ABA caused the change in transpiration rate.

00:05:21.767 --> 00:05:23.126
Pause here to answer.

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:37.373
As ABA rose from day two, transpiration fell.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:41.373
ABA made potassium ions leave the guard cells,

00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:44.493
water followed by osmosis, the guard cells lost

00:05:44.574 --> 00:05:46.733
turgor, and the stomata closed.

00:05:47.294 --> 00:05:50.093
With the pores closed, less water vapour diffused

00:05:50.254 --> 00:05:50.493
out.

00:05:51.214 --> 00:05:54.413
The classic mistake is saying transpiration fell just

00:05:54.494 --> 00:05:56.013
because the soil was dry.

00:05:56.494 --> 00:05:59.453
It fell because the plant closed its stomata.

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:01.146
Second.

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:04.386
Plant X grows in inland Australia.

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:07.386
Its leaves have a thick waxy cuticle, and

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:10.426
stomata sunken in pits lined with hairs.

00:06:10.747 --> 00:06:14.186
Explain how each feature reduces water loss.

00:06:14.347 --> 00:06:18.666
Then compare these features with ABA-triggered stomatal closure

00:06:18.747 --> 00:06:21.066
as ways of keeping water balance.

00:06:21.227 --> 00:06:22.426
Pause here to answer.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:36.273
The cuticle is waterproof, so less water evaporates

00:06:36.354 --> 00:06:37.233
through the surface.

00:06:37.794 --> 00:06:40.433
The hairy pits trap humid air, so the

00:06:40.514 --> 00:06:43.313
vapour difference across each pore is smaller and

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:44.593
diffusion slows.

00:06:45.234 --> 00:06:47.633
Both features work all the time, with no

00:06:47.714 --> 00:06:48.193
signal.

00:06:48.754 --> 00:06:52.273
Closure switches on under stress and can reverse,

00:06:52.314 --> 00:06:54.833
but it also cuts carbon dioxide intake.

00:06:55.474 --> 00:06:58.193
The classic mistake is naming a feature without

00:06:58.274 --> 00:06:59.233
its mechanism.

00:07:00.434 --> 00:07:03.313
So for any plant water question, first ask:

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:06.353
is this a response, or a built-in feature?

00:07:06.914 --> 00:07:10.433
Then name the mechanism, either ions and osmosis

00:07:10.474 --> 00:07:12.513
in the guard cells, or a feature that

00:07:12.674 --> 00:07:16.193
waterproofs, shrinks, cools or traps humid air.

00:07:16.994 --> 00:07:19.953
Next, you'll practise feedback loop questions from across

00:07:20.034 --> 00:07:20.753
this topic.

00:07:21.967 --> 00:07:23.406
Here are the key notes.

00:07:23.647 --> 00:07:25.566
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:25.647 --> 00:07:26.446
to copy them.
